You probably mean Lettuce vs Jedis in Redis caching.

Both are Java clients used to connect Spring Boot/Java applications with Redis.

Jedis vs Lettuce
| Feature             | Jedis                                       | Lettuce                                   |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Type                | Synchronous/blocking                        | Asynchronous + reactive                   |
| Thread Safety       | Not thread-safe (older versions especially) | Thread-safe                               |
| Connection Handling | Usually uses connection pool                | Single shared connection possible         |
| Reactive Support    | No                                          | Yes                                       |
| Async Support       | Limited                                     | Strong support                            |
| Performance         | Good for simple apps                        | Better for scalable/high concurrency apps |
| Spring Boot Default | Older Spring versions                       | Modern Spring Boot default                |
| Complexity          | Simple                                      | Slightly advanced                         |

1. Jedis

Jedis is the older and simpler Redis Java client.

Example:

Jedis jedis = new Jedis("localhost", 6379);
jedis.set("name", "Ali");
String value = jedis.get("name");
Pros
Easy to understand
Lightweight
Good for small/simple applications
Cons
Blocking I/O
Connection pooling needed in multithreaded apps
Less suitable for reactive systems
2. Lettuce

Lettuce is the modern Redis client built on Netty.

Example:

RedisClient client = RedisClient.create("redis://localhost:6379");
StatefulRedisConnection<String, String> connection = client.connect();

RedisCommands<String, String> sync = connection.sync();
sync.set("name", "Ali");
Pros
Thread-safe
Async + reactive support
Better for microservices/high traffic
Scales better
Cons
Slightly more complex
More features than needed for tiny apps
Which One Does Spring Boot Prefer?

Modern Spring Boot uses Lettuce by default.

If you add:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId>
</dependency>

Spring Boot automatically configures Lettuce unless you explicitly switch to Jedis.

Interview Answer (Short Version)

Jedis and Lettuce are Java Redis clients.
Jedis is synchronous and simpler, while Lettuce is thread-safe and supports async/reactive programming.
Modern Spring Boot uses Lettuce by default because it performs better in high-concurrency applications.

Important Interview Point
Why Lettuce is preferred in microservices?

Because:

Non-blocking
Handles many concurrent requests efficiently
Supports reactive programming
Better connection management
Real Project Usage

In Spring Boot caching:

@Cacheable("users")
public User getUser(Long id) {
    return repository.findById(id);
}

Redis backend is commonly powered by Lettuce internally.